fragments":[{"text":"init","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"(","kind":"text"},{"text":"content","kind":"externalParam"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLHarmony","preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL10MXLHarmonyV","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"text":"Chord","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL10MXLHarmonyV5ChordV"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"text":"Content","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL10MXLHarmonyV5ChordV7ContentO"},{"text":", ","kind":"text"},{"text":"kind","kind":"externalParam"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Kind","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL10MXLHarmonyV5ChordV4KindV"},{"text":", ","kind":"text"},{"text":"inversion","kind":"externalParam"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLInversion","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL12MXLInversionV"},{"text":"?, ","kind":"text"},{"text":"bass","kind":"externalParam"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLBass","preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL7MXLBassV","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"?, ","kind":"text"},{"text":"degree","kind":"externalParam"},{"text":": [","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLDegree","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL9MXLDegreeV"},{"text":"])","kind":"text"}]},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL":{"type":"topic","title":"IvorMXL","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A MusicXML parser, normalizer, validator, and formatter."}],"role":"collection","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLBass":{"type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The bass type is used to indicate a bass note in popular music chord symbols, e.g. G\/C. It is generally not"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"used in functional harmony, as inversion is generally not used in pop chord symbols. As with root, it is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"divided into step and alter elements, similar to pitches. The arrangement attribute specifies where the bass"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"is displayed relative to what precedes it."}],"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlbass","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLBass"}],"kind":"symbol","title":"MXLBass","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLBass"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLBass","role":"symbol"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLDegree":{"type":"topic","title":"MXLDegree","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLDegree","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The degree type is used to add, alter, or subtract individual notes in the chord. The print-object attribute"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"can be used to keep the degree from printing separately when it has already taken into account in the text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"attribute of the kind element. The degree-value and degree-type text attributes specify how the value and type"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"of the degree should be displayed."}],"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLDegree"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLDegree"}],"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxldegree"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLInversion":{"type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The inversion type represents harmony inversions. The value is a number indicating which inversion is used: 0"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"for root position, 1 for first inversion, etc. The text attribute indicates how the inversion should be"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"displayed in a score."}],"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlinversion","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLInversion"}],"kind":"symbol","title":"MXLInversion","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLInversion"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLInversion","role":"symbol"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLHarmony":{"type":"topic","title":"MXLHarmony","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLHarmony","abstract":[{"text":"The harmony type represents harmony analysis, including chord symbols in popular music as well as functional","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"harmony analysis in classical music.","type":"text"}],"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLHarmony","kind":"identifier"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MXLHarmony","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlharmony"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLHarmony/Chord-swift.struct/Content-swift.enum":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLHarmony\/Chord-swift.struct\/Content-swift.enum","role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"Content"}],"type":"topic","title":"MXLHarmony.Chord.Content","kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlharmony\/chord-swift.struct\/content-swift.enum","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"One of the alternatives for HarmonyChord."}],"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"enum"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"Content"}]},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLHarmony/Chord-swift.struct":{"type":"topic","title":"MXLHarmony.Chord","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLHarmony\/Chord-swift.struct","abstract":[{"text":"A harmony element can contain many stacked chords (e.g. V of II). A sequence of harmony-chord groups is used","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"for this type of secondary function, where V of II would be represented by a harmony-chord with a 5 numeral","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"followed by a harmony-chord with a 2 numeral.","type":"text"}],"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"Chord"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"Chord"}],"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlharmony\/chord-swift.struct"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLHarmony/Chord-swift.struct/Kind-swift.struct":{"type":"topic","title":"MXLHarmony.Chord.Kind","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLHarmony\/Chord-swift.struct\/Kind-swift.struct","abstract":[{"text":"Kind indicates the type of chord. Degree elements can then add, subtract, or alter from these starting points.","type":"text"}],"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"Kind"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"Kind"}],"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlharmony\/chord-swift.struct\/kind-swift.struct"}}}